WEBVTT

00:48:57.204 --> 00:49:02.913
and leave these people. And there's always
an answer if you're willing to dig

00:49:02.913 --> 00:49:03.913
for it.

00:49:04.246 --> 00:49:07.746
While you were working on Wilde, you
also started writing your column,

00:49:07.746 --> 00:49:09.704
Dear Sugar for the Rumpus.

00:49:10.038 --> 00:49:12.996
You were mentoring students at the
Attic Institute in Portland.

00:49:12.996 --> 00:49:16.204
You were teaching workshops at
universities, writing for magazines.

00:49:16.538 --> 00:49:20.371
But you and Brian, your husband, who's
a documentary filmmaker, were,

00:49:20.371 --> 00:49:26.871
as you put it, epically broke. You were,
as they say, the classic starving artists.

00:49:27.329 --> 00:49:30.871
Yes. And I also thought it was
interesting that, well,